# Multis: * \+ \= \? \{n,m} and the lazy \{-n,m}, empty matches, \@> and greedy versus lazy.

=== * matches as many as possible
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  Matches 0 or more of the preceding atom, as many as possible.
given:  |baaac
keys:   :s/ba*/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(baaa)c

=== * matches zero times
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  a*	   a\*		"", "a", "aa", "aaa", etc.
given:  |bc
keys:   :s/ba*c/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(bc)

=== * at the start of the pattern is a literal star
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  Exception: When "*" is used at the start of the pattern or just after "^" it matches the star character.
given:  |a*b
keys:   :s/*/(&)/<CR>
expect: |a(*)b

=== * just after ^ is a literal star
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  Exception: When "*" is used at the start of the pattern or just after "^" it matches the star character.
given:  |*a*
keys:   :s/^*/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(*)a*

=== an empty match with * is replaced between characters
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  a*	   a\*		"", "a", "aa", "aaa", etc.
given:  |abc
keys:   :s/x*/-/g<CR>
expect: |-a-b-c

=== .* stops at the end of the line
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  .*	   \.\*		anything, also an empty string, no end-of-line
given:
  |a=1
  b=2
keys:   :%s/=.*/(&)/<CR>
expect:
  a(=1)
  |b(=2)

=== \_.* matches to the end of the buffer
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  \_.*	   \_.\*	everything up to the end of the buffer
given:
  |a=1
  b=2
keys:   :%s/=\_.*/X/<CR>
expect:
  |aX

=== \_.*END matches up to the last END
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  \_.*END	   \_.\*END	everything up to and including the last "END" in the buffer
given:
  |a END
  b END c
keys:   :%s/a\_.*END/X/<CR>
expect:
  |X c

=== \+ matches one or more
ref:    pattern.txt */\+*
quote:  Matches 1 or more of the preceding atom, as many as possible.
given:  |b ab aab
keys:   :s/a\+b/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |b (ab) (aab)

=== ^.\+$ matches a non-empty line
ref:    pattern.txt */\+*
quote:  ^.\+$		any non-empty line
given:
  |a

  b
keys:   :%s/^.\+$/(&)/<CR>
expect:
  (a)

  |(b)

=== \= matches zero or one
ref:    pattern.txt */\=*
quote:  foo\=		"fo" and "foo"
given:  |fo foo fooo
keys:   :s/foo\=/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(fo) (foo) (foo)o

=== \? is the same as \=
ref:    pattern.txt */\?*
quote:  Just like \=.
given:  |fo foo
keys:   :s/foo\?/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(fo) (foo)

=== \{n,m} matches n to m times
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  ab\{2,3}c		"abbc" or "abbbc"
given:  |abc abbc abbbc abbbbc
keys:   :s/ab\{2,3}c/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |abc (abbc) (abbbc) abbbbc

=== \{n} matches exactly n times
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  a\{5}			"aaaaa"
given:  |aaaaaaa
keys:   :s/a\{5}/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(aaaaa)aa

=== \{n,} matches at least n times
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  ab\{2,}c		"abbc", "abbbc", "abbbbc", etc.
given:  |abc abbbbc
keys:   :s/ab\{2,}c/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |abc (abbbbc)

=== \{,m} matches at most m times
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  ab\{,3}c		"ac", "abc", "abbc" or "abbbc"
given:  |ac abbbc abbbbc
keys:   :s/ab\{,3}c/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(ac) (abbbc) abbbbc

=== \{} is the same as *
ref:    pattern.txt */\{*
quote:  Matches 0 or more of the preceding atom, as many as possible (like *)
given:  |baaac
keys:   :s/ba\{}/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(baaa)c

=== \{n,m\} may close with a backslash
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  The } may optionally be preceded with a backslash: \{n,m\}.
given:  |aaa
keys:   :s/a\{2\}/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(aa)a

=== \{n} on a group
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  a\(bc\)\{1,2}d		"abcd" or "abcbcd"
given:  |abcd abcbcd abcbcbcd
keys:   :s/a\(bc\)\{1,2}d/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(abcd) (abcbcd) abcbcbcd

=== \{-} matches as few as possible
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  a[bc]\{-}[cd]		"abc" in "abcd"
given:  |abcd
keys:   :s/a[bc]\{-}[cd]/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(abc)d

=== * against \{-} on the same text
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  a[bc]*[cd]		"abcd" in "abcd"
given:  |abcd
keys:   :s/a[bc]*[cd]/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(abcd)

=== \{-} prefers a match that starts earlier
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  A match that starts earlier is preferred over a shorter match: "a\{-}b" matches "aaab" in "xaaab".
given:  |xaaab
keys:   :s/a\{-}b/(&)/<CR>
expect: |x(aaab)

=== .\{-} is a lazy .*
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  In particular, "\{-}" is the same as "*" but uses the shortest match first algorithm.
given:  |<a><b>
keys:   :s/<.\{-}>/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(<a>)(<b>)

=== .* is greedy
ref:    pattern.txt */star*
quote:  Matches 0 or more of the preceding atom, as many as possible.
given:  |<a><b>
keys:   :s/<.*>/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(<a><b>)

=== \{-1,} matches once when it can
ref:    pattern.txt */\{-*
quote:  matches at least n of the preceding atom, as few as possible
given:  |aaa
keys:   :s/a\{-1,}/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(a)(a)(a)

=== \{-n,m} takes n when that is enough
ref:    pattern.txt */\{-*
quote:  matches n to m of the preceding atom, as few as possible
given:  |aaaaa
keys:   :s/a\{-2,4}/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(aa)(aa)a

=== \{-,m} can match nothing
ref:    pattern.txt */\{-*
quote:  matches 0 to m of the preceding atom, as few as possible
given:  |bab
keys:   :s/a\{-,2}b/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(b)(ab)

=== \@> does not give back
ref:    pattern.txt */\@>*
quote:  \(a*\)\@>a	nothing (the "a*" takes all the "a"'s, there can't be another one following)
given:  |aab
keys:   :s/\(a*\)\@>a\|b/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |aa(b)

=== \@> matches when what follows still fits
ref:    pattern.txt */\@>*
quote:  Matches the preceding atom like matching a whole pattern.
given:  |aab
keys:   :s/\(a*\)\@>b/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(aab)

=== a multi on a group repeats the whole group
ref:    pattern.txt *non-greedy*
quote:  a[bc]\{3}d		"abbbd", "abbcd", "acbcd", "acccd", etc.
given:  |abbcd abcd
keys:   :s/a[bc]\{3}d/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(abbcd) abcd
